WEBVTT

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:08.760
Now, the functionality of
our game is almost there.

00:00:08.761 --> 00:00:13.720
We can pick up this power-up, and then after
a certain time, the power-up goes away.

00:00:13.721 --> 00:00:16.241
And then when we hit the
enemy, they fly off into the distance.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:21.400
But one of the small problems is that when
the player, when the power-up is set back

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.869
to false, we have no
way of knowing when they

00:00:24.881 --> 00:00:28.160
picked it up and when
it's been turned off.

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:31.526
So we're going to add a
visual indicator to be able to tell

00:00:31.527 --> 00:00:35.760
when we've picked it up and
then when the cooldowns done.

00:00:35.761 --> 00:00:38.228
We're going to go down
to our project window,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.760
down underneath the assets
folder, underneath course library.

00:00:42.761 --> 00:00:47.640
If we go to power-up indicators and open
that, you can see there's a lot of different

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:54.440
options that we can use to be able to
see when we've picked up a power-up.

00:00:54.441 --> 00:00:57.120
Now we're going to select one.

00:00:57.121 --> 00:01:00.700
So I think in this case,
I like selection ring two.

00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:05.560
So I'm just going to
drag it into my hierarchy.

00:01:05.561 --> 00:01:11.520
Now I'm going to rename
this to power-up indicator.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.320
I'm going to fix it scale a bit just
because it's a little bit too small.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:24.400
You can't even see it inside of the player.

00:01:24.401 --> 00:01:28.080
It looks pretty good, it's a little high so
we can actually, let's see if we can bring

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:33.120
it down just closer to the
ground, something like that, cool.

00:01:33.121 --> 00:01:35.073
Now what we're going
to do is we're actually

00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:36.960
going to, in our
inspector, underneath the

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:40.073
power-up indicator, you
can see this check box

00:01:40.085 --> 00:01:43.960
next to the name and next to the blue
cube, right at the top of the inspector.

00:01:43.961 --> 00:01:50.440
If we actually uncheck that, so it
actually sets that game object as inactive.

00:01:50.441 --> 00:01:53.360
So when we turn it on, we
can see it when we turn it off.

00:01:53.361 --> 00:01:55.783
It disappears, so
that's just kind of useful

00:01:55.795 --> 00:01:59.960
whenever we have different objects
that we don't need to show right now.

00:02:00.134 --> 00:02:03.241
In this case, like the
power-up indicator,

00:02:03.253 --> 00:02:07.520
we only need to know when
we have the actual power-up.

00:02:07.521 --> 00:02:09.520
So that's when we turn it on.

00:02:09.521 --> 00:02:14.720
Now what we're going to do is in our player
controller script, we're actually going to

00:02:14.721 --> 00:02:18.720
create a reference to
that power-up indicator.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.574
We need to create a
public so we can drag it

00:02:26.586 --> 00:02:30.760
into the editor game
object, power-up indicator,

00:02:30.761 --> 00:02:33.494
and then before we
get too far, because I

00:02:33.506 --> 00:02:36.520
make this mistake too
often, in Unity, if you

00:02:36.545 --> 00:02:43.080
click on the player, just make sure to
drag the power-up indicator directly in.

00:02:43.081 --> 00:02:49.440
So now we know that they're attached.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.137
Now, in our code, since
we have that power-up

00:02:51.149 --> 00:02:55.720
indicator, when we pick up the
power-up, we should know that it's on.

00:02:55.721 --> 00:03:03.160
So if we type power-up indicator, and we
get its game object, so you can actually use

00:03:03.161 --> 00:03:07.880
a method to set something
as active or inactive.

00:03:07.881 --> 00:03:11.648
So since it was
inactive in our editor, in

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:17.320
Unity, we can just set this
to true, and it'll turn back on.

00:03:17.321 --> 00:03:22.240
And then in our power-up countdown routine,
since this is where we turn has power-up to

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:31.600
false, we can set power-up indicator,
game object, and set this active false.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:33.880
So this will turn it back off.

00:03:33.881 --> 00:03:39.680
So we'll just make sure
that works really quick.

00:03:39.681 --> 00:03:42.920
We go pick up the power-up.

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:46.880
You can see the indicator on the ground.

00:03:46.881 --> 00:03:50.960
And then when has power-up
is false, and it turns off.

00:03:50.961 --> 00:03:54.200
So one last thing we need to do, it looked
a little weird, because it wasn't actually

00:03:54.201 --> 00:03:55.680
following the player,
it was just on the

00:03:55.692 --> 00:03:59.600
ground, so we can make it even
cooler by having it follow the player.

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:05.400
So in our player controller script, if we
go to our update method, we can actually set

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:23.440
the position of the power-up
indicator to the player's position.

00:04:23.441 --> 00:04:26.751
Now when we press
play, you can see when we

00:04:26.763 --> 00:04:32.320
pick up the power-up that our
indicator turns on on our player.

00:04:32.321 --> 00:04:35.077
So there is one problem,
so this will set the

00:04:35.089 --> 00:04:37.920
position to be exactly
the same as our sphere.

00:04:37.921 --> 00:04:42.000
So we actually just need
to offset it just a little bit.

00:04:42.001 --> 00:04:47.120
So we'll just use a vector 3 for now.

00:04:47.121 --> 00:04:51.017
And we don't want to
move it on the x, we do

00:04:51.029 --> 00:04:58.440
want to bring it a bit lower on the y,
but then we keep it the same on the z.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.748
And now in our game,
you press play, pick up

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.760
the power-up, you can
see that our indicator

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:15.840
is following us on the ground, hit that
enemy out of there, goes away, perfect.

00:05:15.841 --> 00:05:18.539
So what you're going
to do is in our course

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:21.640
library, make sure you
go to power-up indicators,

00:05:21.641 --> 00:05:24.924
drag one of the indicators
into the hierarchy,

00:05:24.936 --> 00:05:28.160
make sure that you set
the power-up indicator

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:33.200
to inactive and just make sure
to rename it so it's easier to find.

00:05:33.201 --> 00:05:35.482
In our player controller
script, we're going

00:05:35.494 --> 00:05:39.160
to make a public game object
for the power-up indicator.

00:05:39.161 --> 00:05:43.155
And then we can just
drag the power-up indicator

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:46.840
from our project
hierarchy to that variable,

00:05:46.841 --> 00:05:49.157
make it so that in your
update method, the

00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:53.880
power-up indicator actually follows
the player as they move around.

00:05:54.055 --> 00:05:56.878
In on trigger-enter,
make sure to set the

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:02.960
power-up indicator to active
using the set-active method.

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:09.840
And then in the power-up countdown
routine, make sure to set active to false.

00:06:09.841 --> 00:06:10.841
Now it's your turn.